<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2004</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004 was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> law that vested ownership of the public foreshore and seabed in the Crown, after a Court of Appeal ruling in 2003 had opened the way for Māori customary title claims. It provoked a large Māori protest march on Parliament and led to the founding of the Māori Party. It was repealed and replaced by the Marine and Coastal Area Act in 2011.</p></div>
